VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-027
Reason for Visit: Acute severe panic attack, flashbacks, and suicidal ideation in a combat veteran.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a veteran experiencing severe, unremitting flashbacks, extreme agitation, and a profound crisis of safety following a triggering event (loud fireworks in the neighborhood). Urgent assessment was required to de-escalate the acute PTSD crisis, ensure immediate physical safety, and assess suicide risk prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't stop seeing the blast, the ringing in my ears is deafening, and I just want it all to stop permanently."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden, violent onset of severe anxiety, hyperarousal, and vivid visual/auditory flashbacks over the past 3 hours, triggered by neighborhood fireworks. Key symptoms stem from chronic, treatment-resistant post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) linked to combat. The patient has a known Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI) presenting with chronic headaches, which are currently exacerbated. There is severe, chronic musculoskeletal pain in the lower back from service injuries. The patient exhibits high rates of severe depression and elevated suicide risk. The spouse reports the patient has been using alcohol heavily as a coping mechanism. The patient complains of severe noise-induced sensorineural hearing loss and chronic, screaming tinnitus that worsens during panic attacks. The patient describes severe insomnia and frequent nightmares, contributing to exhaustion.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely immobilized by severe psychological trauma and panic
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of suicide, firearms present in the home, heavy alcohol use, severe marital strain

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/95 | HR: 125 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Pacing aggressively, profoundly diaphoretic, hypervigilant, startling at minor noises, poor eye contact
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals a fine tremor and severe psychomotor agitation. The patient frequently rubs their temples (chronic headache/tinnitus). Cardiovascular exam shows marked sinus tachycardia consistent with a massive sympathetic surge. Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: combat-related PTSD (active flashbacks), history of TBI, and noise-induced hearing loss/tinnitus.
Environmental Observations: All blinds are drawn tightly; the patient has positioned themselves in a corner with a clear view of the door. The spouse has proactively locked the gun safe and hidden the keys.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been non-compliant with prescribed SSRI (Sertraline) and Prazosin for nightmares for the last month.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Veteran health issues (Acute PTSD Exacerbation / Panic Attack) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Acute Psychotic Break (Primary Schizophrenia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hallucinations/flashbacks are strictly tied to specific, verifiable combat trauma rather than bizarre, non-congruent delusions.
2. Alcohol Withdrawal Delirium (Delirium Tremens) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sudden, trigger-specific onset (fireworks) and the absence of global confusion or disorientation to person/place.
3. Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of central crushing chest pain, with the tachycardia and hypertension being directly proportional to the severe, observable psychological panic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of extreme sympathetic hyperarousal and acute psychological crisis, experiencing active combat flashbacks and expressing active suicidal ideation, triggered by environmental noise. Delaying this assessment would have left the patient at an unacceptably high risk of completing suicide or causing accidental harm during a dissociative flashback. The objective findings of severe tachycardia, extreme agitation, and the necessity for immediate, specialized safety planning (securing firearms, de-escalation) completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to stabilize the veteran and prevent a fatal outcome.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate verbal de-escalation and grounding techniques (e.g., 5-4-3-2-1 sensory method). Provision of a single dose of oral Diazepam to rapidly reduce the acute sympathetic surge and agitation. Confirmation with the spouse that all firearms are secured and inaccessible.
Positive Behavioral Change: By practicing the grounding breathing exercises we just did every time the tinnitus gets loud, you give your brain a physical anchor to the present moment, which stops the flashback from taking over your nervous system.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to Open Arms (Veterans & Families Counseling) and the local mental health crisis team.
DME/Safety (Optional): None
Follow-up: Mandatory GP and psychiatric review within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 (Police/Ambulance) if the patient attempts to access weapons, becomes violent, or if the suicidal urges become uncontrollable.

